```ts
function useCanExecute(documentId: string | null | undefined, candidates: ActionCandidate[],
                       branch?: string /* "main" */, subject?: AuthSubject /* signed-in user */): CanExecuteState;

type CanExecuteState = {
  status: "idle" | "loading" | "ready" | "unsupported" | "error";
  evaluations?: Evaluation[]; allAllowed?: boolean; anyAllowed?: boolean;
  allDenied?: boolean; anyDenied?: boolean; error?: Error; refetch: () => void;
};
```

`status: "unsupported"` **is not a denial.** It means this deployment answers no authorization preflight — no reactor client module is set, or the reactor runs without `authEnforcement` — so render controls as you would have without asking. Treating it as a denial disables every control on a deployment that never enabled enforcement. The idiom is `const disabled = status === "ready" && !allAllowed`, with `evaluations[0].reason` as the tooltip.

Behaviour worth relying on: the hook **waits for the signed-in identity to settle** before evaluating, so a toolbar does not grey itself on first paint and then ungrey; it **re-evaluates when the document's revision moves**; and candidates are read **by content rather than identity**, so an inline array does not re-request on every render. Two gaps are documented: a `{ group }` grant depends on a roster held in a different document, whose membership change fires no event for this one; and a change event carries no scope, so *any* write to the document re-evaluates.

On the light GraphQL client (`GraphQLReactorProvider`) the hook always reports `unsupported`: it reads the reactor client module, which that setup never sets, and `evaluateActions` is not on `IReactorBrowserClient`. Controls render as if unasked; the submit still refuses. As with the underlying call, an allow means the control is worth offering, not that the write will succeed — keep handling the failure from `dispatch`.

